            "result_text": "Done! The regex has been saved to `/app/regex.txt`.\n\n**How the pattern works:**\n\n1. **IPv4 lookahead** `(?=.*\\b<ipv4>\\b)` — Uses a positive lookahead to ensure an IPv4 address (with word boundaries) exists on the line without consuming it\n\n2. **Greedy matching** `.*` — Matches everything up to the last date on the line (greedy means it reaches the furthest possible point)\n\n3. **Date capture** `\\b([0-9]{4}-(?:0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(?:0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|3[01]))\\b` — Captures the date in parentheses:\n   - Year: `[0-9]{4}`\n   - Month: `(?:0[1-9]|1[0-2])` (01-12)\n   - Day: `(?:0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|3[01])` (01-31, including Feb 29)\n   - Word boundaries prevent partial matches\n\n4. **IPv4 octet pattern** `(?:25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|1[0-9]{2}|[1-9]?[0-9])` — Matches 0-255 without leading zeros:\n   - 250-255, 200-249, 100-199, 0-99\n\n5. **Negative lookahead** `(?!.*\\bDATE\\b)` — Ensures this is the last date on the line\n\nWith `re.MULTILINE`, the `.` doesn't match newlines, so matching stays within single lines. `re.findall()` returns only the captured date group."
